\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Comparison of changed and unchanged decision rules on a $64\times 64$ grid with an unlimited dealer and edge traders responding to price. Transition probability is the fraction of runs in which at least half of the interior traders decided to sell after the rule change. Median onset is the median time to reach this condition among runs that did so; it is undefined where no run reached a majority.}}{14}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:paired-switch}{{S2}{14}{Comparison of changed and unchanged decision rules on a $64\times 64$ grid with an unlimited dealer and edge traders responding to price. Transition probability is the fraction of runs in which at least half of the interior traders decided to sell after the rule change. Median onset is the median time to reach this condition among runs that did so; it is undefined where no run reached a majority}{table.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Effect of grid size over 50 starting markets and 3,000 steps after the rule change. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from seed resampling. $n_{\rm  int}$ counts fractions with $0.05<\hat  p<0.95$. Crossings use linear interpolation. The susceptibility maximum is over the sampled grid, and its interval includes reselection of that maximum within each bootstrap replicate. Intervals condition on the refined grid.}}{14}{table.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:finite-size-scaling}{{S3}{14}{Effect of grid size over 50 starting markets and 3,000 steps after the rule change. Brackets give 95\% confidence intervals (CI) from seed resampling. $n_{\rm int}$ counts fractions with $0.05<\hat p<0.95$. Crossings use linear interpolation. The susceptibility maximum is over the sampled grid, and its interval includes reselection of that maximum within each bootstrap replicate. Intervals condition on the refined grid}{table.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares, over 50 starting markets. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer capacity. Keeping edge traders in Hold gave the same probability for all combinations; allowing them to respond to price gave the stated range.}}{14}{table.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:balance-fractions}{{S4}{14}{Probability of a Sell majority after changes in trader cash and shares, over 50 starting markets. Each fraction includes 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer capacity. Keeping edge traders in Hold gave the same probability for all combinations; allowing them to respond to price gave the stated range}{table.caption.31}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Asking selected traders to sell once (Sell pulse) versus keeping Rule~19286 in use. Both comparisons used the same traders and 3,000 steps after the change. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals; the dealer was unlimited under both boundary conditions.}}{15}{table.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-pulse}{{S5}{15}{Asking selected traders to sell once (Sell pulse) versus keeping Rule~19286 in use. Both comparisons used the same traders and 3,000 steps after the change. Entries are majority probabilities with 95\% Wilson intervals; the dealer was unlimited under both boundary conditions}{table.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces All changes to one response in Rule~19280. Each probability pair is edge traders kept in Hold / responding to price over 20 starting markets; the changed rule remained in use. The output labels are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, Sell $=2$.}}{15}{table.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-rules}{{S6}{15}{All changes to one response in Rule~19280. Each probability pair is edge traders kept in Hold / responding to price over 20 starting markets; the changed rule remained in use. The output labels are Buy $=0$, Hold $=1$, Sell $=2$}{table.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S7}{\ignorespaces Adjusting versus fixed widths of the Hold range at $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI). Sell is the mean fraction of active edge traders in Sell, averaged over steps after the rule change and starting markets.}}{15}{table.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-band}{{S7}{15}{Adjusting versus fixed widths of the Hold range at $f=0.175$ over 50 starting markets. Probability brackets are 95\% Wilson confidence intervals (CI). Sell is the mean fraction of active edge traders in Sell, averaged over steps after the rule change and starting markets}{table.caption.34}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Changes in how strongly trades affected price at $f=0.20$, with edge traders responding to price, 50 seeds per cell. Drawdown is a percentage; rejection is the fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected.}}{16}{table.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-impact}{{S8}{16}{Changes in how strongly trades affected price at $f=0.20$, with edge traders responding to price, 50 seeds per cell. Drawdown is a percentage; rejection is the fraction of post-switch eligible orders rejected}{table.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S9}{\ignorespaces Changes in observation time and the required Sell fraction, with edge traders kept in Hold, over 50 starting markets. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ [95\% seed-bootstrap interval] for each crossing criterion. The horizon grids are independent of adaptive refinement, so their interpolated values can differ slightly from Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:finite-size-scaling}.}}{16}{table.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-horizons}{{S9}{16}{Changes in observation time and the required Sell fraction, with edge traders kept in Hold, over 50 starting markets. Entries are interpolated $f_{50}$ [95\% seed-bootstrap interval] for each crossing criterion. The horizon grids are independent of adaptive refinement, so their interpolated values can differ slightly from Supplementary Table~\ref {tab:finite-size-scaling}}{table.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S10}{\ignorespaces Comparison of logistic fits with interpolation between tested fractions. Parentheses give seed-bootstrap standard errors (SE), not binomial standard errors treating fractions as independent. The last column is the location of the empirical susceptibility maximum: it is a sampled fraction with local grid resolution, not an interpolated optimum.}}{16}{table.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:revision-fits}{{S10}{16}{Comparison of logistic fits with interpolation between tested fractions. Parentheses give seed-bootstrap standard errors (SE), not binomial standard errors treating fractions as independent. The last column is the location of the empirical susceptibility maximum: it is a sampled fraction with local grid resolution, not an interpolated optimum}{table.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Changing the decision rule caused initially identical markets to develop differently. Fifty starting markets were simulated on a $64\times 64$ grid for 1,000 steps before the rule change and 3,000 steps afterward. Each comparison market kept the original rule at the same selected sites. \textbf  {(A)}~Median price divided by its value when the rule changed; bands mark the interquartile range. \textbf  {(B)}~Median fraction of interior traders in Sell; the horizontal line marks one-half. \textbf  {(C)}~Snapshots of decisions for seed 12, the $f=0.20$ pair nearest the median difference in peak Sell fraction between changed and unchanged markets. Blue, gray, and orange cells denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. Rows follow the two markets from their common state when the rule was assigned through 3,000 steps. All paired markets were identical before the vertical line marking the rule change.}}{17}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rule-quench}{{1}{17}{Changing the decision rule caused initially identical markets to develop differently. Fifty starting markets were simulated on a $64\times 64$ grid for 1,000 steps before the rule change and 3,000 steps afterward. Each comparison market kept the original rule at the same selected sites. \textbf {(A)}~Median price divided by its value when the rule changed; bands mark the interquartile range. \textbf {(B)}~Median fraction of interior traders in Sell; the horizontal line marks one-half. \textbf {(C)}~Snapshots of decisions for seed 12, the $f=0.20$ pair nearest the median difference in peak Sell fraction between changed and unchanged markets. Blue, gray, and orange cells denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. Rows follow the two markets from their common state when the rule was assigned through 3,000 steps. All paired markets were identical before the vertical line marking the rule change}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Changing decision rules while varying how much the dealer could buy and sell. \textbf  {(A)}~Probability of a Sell majority with edge traders kept in Hold and with those traders responding to price at selected dealer capacities. These conditions are labeled held boundary and endogenous boundary, respectively. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf  {(B)}~Difference in majority probability between the condition with edge traders responding to price and the condition with them kept in Hold, across all capacities. \textbf  {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at $f=0.20$; bands mark the interquartile range. \textbf  {(D)}~Median fraction of orders backed by trader cash or shares that the dealer rejected at $f=0.20$. Capacity $h=10$ appears as a dashed overlay in (A) and a separate capacity in (B--D); it matched $h=\infty $ exactly.}}{18}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reservoir}{{2}{18}{Changing decision rules while varying how much the dealer could buy and sell. \textbf {(A)}~Probability of a Sell majority with edge traders kept in Hold and with those traders responding to price at selected dealer capacities. These conditions are labeled held boundary and endogenous boundary, respectively. Bands are 95\% Wilson intervals over 50 starting markets. \textbf {(B)}~Difference in majority probability between the condition with edge traders responding to price and the condition with them kept in Hold, across all capacities. \textbf {(C)}~Median maximum drawdown at $f=0.20$; bands mark the interquartile range. \textbf {(D)}~Median fraction of orders backed by trader cash or shares that the dealer rejected at $f=0.20$. Capacity $h=10$ appears as a dashed overlay in (A) and a separate capacity in (B--D); it matched $h=\infty $ exactly}{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Effect of market size on the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets. \textbf  {(A)}~Majority probability with edge traders kept in Hold; bands are 95\% Wilson intervals. \textbf  {(B)}~Variation between runs with edge traders kept in Hold, measured as susceptibility: the variance of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf  {(C)}~Fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the runs reached a majority, estimated by interpolation for edge traders kept in Hold and for two dealer capacities with edge traders responding to price. \textbf  {(D)}~Maximum susceptibility versus grid size on linear axes. Error bars in (C,D) are 95\% intervals from resampling starting markets. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in (A) are logistic fits. No law for the limit of indefinitely large grids was fitted.}}{19}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:finite-size}{{3}{19}{Effect of market size on the formation of a Sell majority over 50 starting markets. \textbf {(A)}~Majority probability with edge traders kept in Hold; bands are 95\% Wilson intervals. \textbf {(B)}~Variation between runs with edge traders kept in Hold, measured as susceptibility: the variance of the final 500-step mean Sell fraction multiplied by the number of interior traders. \textbf {(C)}~Fraction $f_{50}(L)$ at which half the runs reached a majority, estimated by interpolation for edge traders kept in Hold and for two dealer capacities with edge traders responding to price. \textbf {(D)}~Maximum susceptibility versus grid size on linear axes. Error bars in (C,D) are 95\% intervals from resampling starting markets. Solid lines connect tested values; dashed curves in (A) are logistic fits. No law for the limit of indefinitely large grids was fitted}{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces How traders' decisions changed over time when different fractions received the new rule, with edge traders responding to price and an unlimited dealer. Blue, gray, and orange cells denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf  {(A)}~Decisions in five runs (columns) when the rule changed and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps (rows). From left to right, the runs include a case without a majority at $f=0.15$, both outcomes at $f=0.175$, and cases with a majority at $f=0.185$ and 0.20. Runs with a majority were selected by proximity to their group's median time to a majority. Runs without a majority were selected by proximity to their group's median final Sell fraction. Selection preceded inspection of images. Seeds were 8, 5, 22, 46, and 21 from left to right. \textbf  {(B)}~Price divided by its value when the rule changed. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction; the horizontal line marks one-half. Vertical guides mark the four snapshots.}}{20}{figure.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dose-panels}{{4}{20}{How traders' decisions changed over time when different fractions received the new rule, with edge traders responding to price and an unlimited dealer. Blue, gray, and orange cells denote Buy, Hold, and Sell. \textbf {(A)}~Decisions in five runs (columns) when the rule changed and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps (rows). From left to right, the runs include a case without a majority at $f=0.15$, both outcomes at $f=0.175$, and cases with a majority at $f=0.185$ and 0.20. Runs with a majority were selected by proximity to their group's median time to a majority. Runs without a majority were selected by proximity to their group's median final Sell fraction. Selection preceded inspection of images. Seeds were 8, 5, 22, 46, and 21 from left to right. \textbf {(B)}~Price divided by its value when the rule changed. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction; the horizontal line marks one-half. Vertical guides mark the four snapshots}{figure.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces How the same starting market developed with different dealer capacities at $f=0.20$. \textbf  {(A)}~Decisions under four conditions (columns) when the rule changed and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps (rows). The first three columns allowed edge traders to respond to price, with dealer capacities $h=0.1$, $h=1$, and $h=\infty $. The last kept edge traders in Hold with $h=\infty $. Seed 25 was selected from runs that reached a majority at all three capacities by minimizing the sum of distances from each capacity's median time to a majority. Rows show the same elapsed times. \textbf  {(B)}~Price divided by its value when the rule changed. \textbf  {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. Keeping edge traders in Hold prevented price from influencing decisions inside the grid; trades and price changes were still calculated. This example follows one starting market, while the full comparison measures outcomes across starting markets (Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir}).}}{21}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dealer-panels}{{5}{21}{How the same starting market developed with different dealer capacities at $f=0.20$. \textbf {(A)}~Decisions under four conditions (columns) when the rule changed and after 500, 1,500, and 3,000 steps (rows). The first three columns allowed edge traders to respond to price, with dealer capacities $h=0.1$, $h=1$, and $h=\infty $. The last kept edge traders in Hold with $h=\infty $. Seed 25 was selected from runs that reached a majority at all three capacities by minimizing the sum of distances from each capacity's median time to a majority. Rows show the same elapsed times. \textbf {(B)}~Price divided by its value when the rule changed. \textbf {(C)}~Interior Sell fraction. Keeping edge traders in Hold prevented price from influencing decisions inside the grid; trades and price changes were still calculated. This example follows one starting market, while the full comparison measures outcomes across starting markets (Fig.~\ref {fig:reservoir})}{figure.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Effects of changing traders' cash and shares when the new decision rule was introduced, with edge traders responding to price, $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets. In A to C, the nine combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a separate series for each dealer capacity. \textbf  {(A)}~Probability of a Sell majority. The dashed line and band give the probability and its 95\% Wilson interval with edge traders kept in Hold, which were identical in all nine combinations because resource changes could not affect decisions. \textbf  {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown on a logarithmic axis. Medians of exactly zero appear at the axis floor with open markers. \textbf  {(C)}~Median fraction of orders backed by trader cash or shares that the dealer rejected. \textbf  {(D)}~Majority probability against median maximum drawdown for all 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer capacity with edge traders responding to price. The axis and zero markers follow B, and the reference probability with edge traders kept in Hold is repeated.}}{22}{figure.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:balance-sheet}{{6}{22}{Effects of changing traders' cash and shares when the new decision rule was introduced, with edge traders responding to price, $f=0.18$, and 50 starting markets. In A to C, the nine combinations are grouped by cash multiplier, with share multiplier on the axis and a separate series for each dealer capacity. \textbf {(A)}~Probability of a Sell majority. The dashed line and band give the probability and its 95\% Wilson interval with edge traders kept in Hold, which were identical in all nine combinations because resource changes could not affect decisions. \textbf {(B)}~Median maximum drawdown on a logarithmic axis. Medians of exactly zero appear at the axis floor with open markers. \textbf {(C)}~Median fraction of orders backed by trader cash or shares that the dealer rejected. \textbf {(D)}~Majority probability against median maximum drawdown for all 27 combinations of cash, shares, and dealer capacity with edge traders responding to price. The axis and zero markers follow B, and the reference probability with edge traders kept in Hold is repeated}{figure.caption.44}{}}
